1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:28,000
How are you guys doing this morning?

2
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Wow, that was not good. How are you all doing this morning? Okay, thank you, thank you. Excited to celebrate baptism today. One thing we believe here at Canvas Community Church, and hopefully in all churches, is that the Holy Spirit still speaks to His people.

3
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:56,000
And as we were baptizing this morning, I had one of our leaders come up and just encourage me to kind of let people know this morning that we're going to open up the baptism at the end of service.

4
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:20,000
So if you have never been baptized, if you believe that Jesus is the Son of God, that He died for your sins to provide forgiveness and reconciliation to the Father, if you are willing to surrender your life to Him as Lord and leader, and you want to demonstrate that through baptism this morning, I don't want that to be an emotional decision, and so we're going to wait until the end.

5
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:44,000
You have about 37 and a half minutes to think about that, to pray about that. And so if that's something that you want to do this morning, you've never done it before and you want to make that decision publicly, we would love to celebrate with you. Don't, again, like I said, I'm not gonna, I'm not gonna have Marissa come up here and play the same hymn over and over and over again until somebody finally says, like, "I'll be baptized again so we can go to lunch." Like, that's not what we're doing.

6
00:01:44,000 --> 00:02:10,000
But if the Spirit is at work in your life and this is a decision that you don't want to wait on, that you are ready to make today. The Scriptures say that today, if you hear His voice, do not harden your heart. And so if you are hearing from the Lord today that it is time for you to surrender your life, I don't care if you were, I don't care if you were passed out drunk at a bar last night, if you are hearing today that it is your day to surrender to the Lord.

7
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:36,000
There is nothing that you need to do beforehand to receive the free gift of salvation from Jesus. You don't need to clean yourself up. You don't need to figure it out. All you need to do is surrender. And so if you're ready to do that, whether you are stumbling in off the street from the bar or whether you've been raised in church your whole life and you have obeyed all the rules and you are just now realizing that obedience to the rules are not enough, whatever that might be for you.

8
00:02:36,000 --> 00:03:02,000
So I'll leave that there for now. We are going to hop into week four of our eight-week series on spiritual practices, the spiritual practices of community and service. And we are doing these series built around the practices of Jesus because we want to be doers of the word and not just hearers or affirmers or agreeers of the word, right? We want to be people who obey the Scriptures, who do what God has invited us to do.

9
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:13,000
And so we're focusing for the next eight weeks on community and service. We're wrapping up that kind of community aspect of the series today, and we will hop into service next week.

10
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:29,000
And what is the point of God drawing us into a family, a community, if that community does not serve each other and outside of ourselves? And so we've tied these two practices together, and we want you in your small groups to be thinking about how God might be inviting you to serve as a community.

11
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Whether you're serving within the household of God, whether you know brothers and sisters who are struggling, who need help, who need encouragement, who need some act of service, whether you want to serve inside the community or you want to go out into our community, people who might not know Jesus and serve them.

12
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:56,000
We want you to think about ways that you might serve our community. And there's a Sunday, and I am a planner. You guys, I don't know if you know that about me or not, but I'm a planner. I like to plan.

13
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:11,000
And there's a Sunday at the end of this series that I have nothing planned for. And I did that on purpose, hoping that you all might have testimonies of the things that God has done in and through you as you've come together into community and look to serve one another.

14
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:21,000
And so if, as you are meeting together in smaller groups and serving and building God's kingdom outside of these walls, if you have testimonies that you want to share, please let us know.

15
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:32,000
We want to do that. We want to honor that, take a whole Sunday to kind of celebrate that. And if we don't have any testimonies to share, then we'll figure it out. All right?

16
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
We got testimonies. All right, Ballinger, you're up for that Sunday. I'll put you on.

17
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:44,000
So, so far we've talked about that God designed his people that we would form tight-knit communities around a table that looks like families.

18
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:52,000
What we read in the passage in 1 Corinthians, we are one body. It is a multicultural, multi-ethnic, multi-economic family of God.

19
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Some are slaves, some are free, some are Jews, some are Gentiles, but we are united by our surrender to King Jesus.

20
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,000
And this community, because it's like family, it should move beyond shallow relationships.

21
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
And many of us that grew up in the church, we know that a lot of church relationships are shallow.

22
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
And so what we want to do here as a community at Canvas is we want to move past those shallow relationships.

23
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:16,000
We want to share our joys and our sorrows with one another.

24
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,000
We want to move towards confessing our sins to one another.

25
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:26,000
This will require lots of time and intentionality, but it will produce the fruit that Jesus promises.

26
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:35,000
And so even if we succeed at doing all the things that we've talked about so far, making a family around the table, sharing our joys and sorrows, confessing our sins,

27
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:45,000
there is one kind of lurking temptation and excuse for us to walk away from community that will stay in the darkness.

28
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:50,000
One more obstacle lurking in the shadow, and that is conflict and disagreement.

29
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:00,000
It's so easy for us to walk away from the community that we form the moment that somebody says something that we don't like to hear or does something that offends us.

30
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:07,000
And what we're gonna talk about this morning is this discipline, this practice of staying in community even when it's hard.

31
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Of remaining committed to unity and community even when difficult things arise, even when emotional conflicts arise.

32
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:25,000
So that's where we're headed. Sound good? Cool. Let's pray. Father in heaven, we are grateful for another opportunity to look at your word this morning.

33
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Open our eyes to see what you have for us. Holy Spirit, speak in the ways that you wanna speak.

34
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,000
Translate my words into whatever words these people need to hear.

35
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:47,000
May each individual feel as if they heard from you, not because I have said anything eloquent or creative, but because the Holy Spirit speaks when the word of God is open.

36
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,000
So do what you wanna do in this place. God, even now as people may be considering baptism,

37
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:58,000
if you are drawing someone to the waters of baptism this morning, make that evidently and abundantly clear in their lives.

38
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
I pray all of this in Jesus' name, amen.

39
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,000
How many of you are familiar with the concept of cancel culture? Anybody?

40
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:12,000
Heard of the term, right? Okay, cancel culture is this idea where somebody does something or says something wrong,

41
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:19,000
or we go back through their Twitter timeline from 15 years ago and find out something they said when they were 13,

42
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:24,000
hyped up on Adderall and Monster, and then we cancel them for that, right?

43
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:31,000
I tried really hard to find a specific example of cancel culture where we take somebody that's kind of in the public eye,

44
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:37,000
and we kick them out of the public eye because of something they said or did or thought or whatever,

45
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:42,000
and I couldn't even find a good example that I thought wouldn't offend at least someone in the room,

46
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,000
because I could pick an example that I thought was silly, and then somebody in the room would be like,

47
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:49,000
no, they should have been canceled for that. What are you talking about? Right?

48
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:56,000
So I didn't pick any specific examples, but I just wanted to bring up this underlying culture that most of us are aware of

49
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:03,000
to point out the fact that I think we live in a world where if someone doesn't like you or disagrees with you,

50
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:07,000
they can discredit you and remove you from the community that you're a part of.

51
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:14,000
And in this culture, even apologies attempts to grow, attempts to say, "Hey, I was wrong. I'm sorry."

52
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:22,000
They're mocked and ridiculed and laughed at, and you're pushed even further out onto the margins.

53
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:29,000
I think this kind of underlying idea of cancel culture reminds us that we live in a culture that is addicted to outrage

54
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:39,000
and allergic to reconciliation. And although this is a culture that most of us live in, we all, or a lot of us,

55
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:45,000
many of us in the room have surrendered to a rabbi who says that they will know the world outside of us,

56
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:50,000
will know that we are his disciples by our love for one another, which means cancel culture

57
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:58,000
and walking away from relationships does not happen in the church. Right?

58
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:04,000
Cancel culture, walking away from relationships when disagreements happen, that does not happen in the church.

59
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:10,000
None of you have heard stories about churches splitting up over what color the carpet was going to be.

60
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,000
We do not participate in this kind of silliness in Christ's church, right?

61
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:20,000
You guys picking up on my sarcasm? Okay, thank you. We're being sarcastic.

62
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:29,000
So, I think my thesis for today is that in a world addicted to outrage and allergic to reconciliation,

63
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:39,000
in a community of people that say we're surrendered to a rabbi who says, "The world outside of these walls will know that you are my disciples by your love for one another."

64
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:45,000
That we have this clear command from Jesus that will set us up as a counter witness to the world around us.

65
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:50,000
This is a perfect opportunity for us to shine a light in the darkness.

66
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:58,000
If we are saying that the culture around us is addicted to outrage and conflict and allergic to reconciliation,

67
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:06,000
then what better witness of the gospel of kingdom of God if his church stays united in the midst of difficulty and disagreement and conflict?

68
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:13,000
So the question becomes, if my thesis is, and this is what the world is like outside the walls of the church,

69
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:17,000
and this is our invitation to shine a light, to be salt to the earth, a city on a hill.

70
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,000
If this is our invitation, the question becomes, will we take advantage of this opportunity?

71
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:29,000
Will we prioritize developing a diverse and unified community that chooses to stay together through significant conflict and disagreement?

72
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:33,000
I'm not talking about carpet color. I don't think our church would split over carpet color.

73
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:40,000
But we are going to have, if we, yeah. You guys haven't even left, and we don't have floors on half the building, so.

74
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:46,000
So I don't think we're going to split over this, but when it comes to significant emotionally charged topics,

75
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:51,000
are we going to commit to remain with one another in community?

76
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:58,000
The invitation of Jesus is to intentionally work towards creating a laboratory of sacrificial love,

77
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:05,000
where we all work towards this idea of laying down our preferences and our priorities in order to stay united with one another,

78
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
even when everything wants us to leave.

79
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:14,000
And instead, I think we often form echo chambers of our own preferences.

80
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:21,000
The invitation is a laboratory of love, and we often settle for an echo chamber of our own preferences.

81
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:27,000
And if we just look at the example of Jesus, he had 12 of his closest followers together.

82
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
They followed him everywhere they went.

83
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
Think about the difficult circumstances of their life on the road.

84
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
And two of those disciples were a tax collector and a zealot.

85
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Could not have had stronger opinions that disagreed with one another.

86
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:43,000
And the invitation to Jesus is you lay down those opinions,

87
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:48,000
and you form a community that is built on me, my body and my blood.

88
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:53,000
So will we take this opportunity?

89
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,000
Or will we take the easy road?

90
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:01,000
One of these options is easy, but will deform us from the image of Jesus.

91
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,000
The other is extremely difficult, but it leads to life.

92
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:16,000
So let's hop into 1 Corinthians 3, where we're going to see Paul kind of get a little bit angry and angsty at some people that have divided for some silly reasons.

93
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:24,000
And we're going to see what we can't learn about staying together in the midst of tense disagreements and difficulty.

94
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
1 Corinthians 3, starting in verse 1.

95
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:38,000
Paul says, "Brothers and sisters, I could not address you as people who live by the Spirit, but as people who are still worldly, mere infants in Christ.

96
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:42,000
I gave you milk, not solid food, for you were not ready for it.

97
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:46,000
Indeed, you still are not ready for it. You are still worldly.

98
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:53,000
For since there is jealousy and quarreling among you, are you not worthy, worldly?

99
00:12:53,000 --> 00:13:02,000
Are you not acting like mere humans? For when one says, "I follow Paul," and another, "I follow Apollos," are you not mere humans?

100
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:08,000
What, after all, is Apollos? And what is Paul? Only servants through whom you came to believe.

101
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:17,000
As the Lord has assigned to each his task, I planted the seed Apollos watered, but God has been making it grow."

102
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:25,000
So Paul here is lovingly rebuking these people who think that they are varsity-level spiritual people.

103
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:33,000
If you were to go to the Corinthian church, you would see gifts of the Spirit happening in some miraculous ways,

104
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:37,000
things that we've only read about as American Christians.

105
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:43,000
They were doing some really powerful experiences, worship, all of this stuff was going on, and they thought they were mature.

106
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:52,000
They thought they were godly because God was using them to accomplish big, powerful, important things, and Paul, as their spiritual father, steps in.

107
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:58,000
And he offers them a loving rebuke. I love that he starts with brothers and sisters right before he calls them babies.

108
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:04,000
All right? That's his pastoral heart coming through. He's like, "Listen, I love you all so, so much. You're infants."

109
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:10,000
You think you are varsity because of your services and your gifts, but you are toddlers at best.

110
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:15,000
And what I find so fascinating about this is, as you go throughout the book of 1 Corinthians,

111
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:20,000
you're gonna find out that there's a million reasons why Paul could have called them immature. Right?

112
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:24,000
There's some pretty significant sexual sin happening in the Corinthian church.

113
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:29,000
There's some pretty horrific things happening with the way the rich people are treating the poor people.

114
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:33,000
There's like some significant sin happening in the Corinth church.

115
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:39,000
And when Paul says you're spiritual infants, you're immature, he doesn't say because of your sexual sin.

116
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:43,000
He doesn't say because of, he says because you're not united with one another.

117
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:49,000
He says because you're splitting off fellowship with one another over who your favorite preacher is.

118
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:58,000
This is nonsense. This is silly. You are refusing to stay united around the thing that matters most, God doing the work in you.

119
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:03,000
And instead you are siphoning off into other little tables based on your favorite preacher.

120
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:11,000
You are creating echo chambers of your own preference when Jesus has invited you to create a laboratory of sacrificial love.

121
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:21,000
He mentions three different teachers, and the point here is that they were kind of picking their favorite packaging of the gospel or their favorite gifting.

122
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:27,000
Paul had a different gifting than Apollos, and Apollos had a different calling than Peter, and they kind of packaged the gospel in different ways.

123
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:31,000
And the reality is, as I was thinking about this this week, is that we still do this today.

124
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:39,000
We have different groups of preachers and different denominations of the way they package the theology.

125
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:45,000
We follow them on YouTube or on social media, and we're like, "This guy, this group of people, they're the ones that are actually preaching the gospel."

126
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:51,000
If I have to hear one more time a Christian come to me and say, "Well, I went to a church because they're actually preaching the Bible."

127
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:56,000
I'm like, "Okay, find me, list 10 churches in our community that aren't preaching the Bible."

128
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:02,000
It's not that they're not preaching the Bible, it's that they're not preaching the Bible the way that you want it to be preached.

129
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:12,000
It's that they're not preaching the Bible based on your preferences, and they're not talking about the sins that you find most bad more often than not.

130
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:17,000
They're probably actually talking about the sins that you struggle with more, and that's why you've decided that you don't want to hear what they have to say.

131
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:23,000
You'd rather go to the person that's going to talk about the people you don't like and pad you from your own disobedience.

132
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:35,000
And Paul says, "No, we all need to be together." We all need to be together because we point out each other's blind spots, and it's God that's doing the work in and through us.

133
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:43,000
And if we pack, if we siphon ourselves off into these smaller communities based on our preferences, we'll end up creating echo chambers that lead to immaturity.

134
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:50,000
And Paul says this, he says, "You are infants." I call this the cycle of immaturity.

135
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:57,000
I think I have it on the screen, but this is ultimately what happens. It still happens to this day is disagreement or conflict arise.

136
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:04,000
And when disagreement and conflict arises in community, we really have one of two options. We can divide or we can stay and reconcile.

137
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:10,000
And what the people in Corinth decided to do is when disagreement and conflict arises, they said, "We're gonna divide.

138
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:14,000
We're gonna go based on the person that we like, the group of people that we like.

139
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:18,000
We're gonna follow this version of the gospel rather than this version of the gospel."

140
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:27,000
And what tends to happen is when we tribalize, we throw rocks at the other side, even when they're supposed to be in our family, one body, right?

141
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:35,000
And so we're throwing rocks at each other, we are disagreeing and we divide, and that division leads to one of two things, uniformity or isolation.

142
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:43,000
So uniformity happens when we divide and we just group together with the people that agree with us about the conflict or disagreement.

143
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:47,000
We're right, they're wrong, can you believe they, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

144
00:17:47,000 --> 00:18:04,000
We're all, it looks like unity. Here's the different, here's the struggle. The enemy of God deceives us, he counterfeits the kingdom, and uniformity looks like unity for a while, but it does not produce the maturity that unity produces.

145
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:16,000
Uniformity produces immaturity because we all think that we're God's gift to creation, we're varsity believers, and we need somebody to step in and say, "Actually, you're still in diapers."

146
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:26,000
And maybe you're mature in this area and this area, but you're in diapers in this area, but if we're all mature in the same area and immature in the other areas, and we gather together around one table, we never talk about the areas we're immature.

147
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:36,000
We only just puff ourselves up based on our maturity in certain areas. You understand what I'm saying? So disagreement and conflict arises, and if we choose division, it will lead to uniformity or isolation.

148
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:45,000
We divide so much. What happens is we get into this group of people that look a lot like us, and that's going well for a while, but human beings are human beings.

149
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:52,000
And like, no matter how much they're like you, they'll still say something that offends you, and then another disagreement arises.

150
00:18:52,000 --> 00:19:02,000
And then you get to choose there. Am I going to stick with this group of people or I'm going to divide? And sometimes we divide and we divide so much that we are by ourselves thinking we're the only ones.

151
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:09,000
Right? Like the prophet Elijah, God, I'm the only one doing the thing. God's like, "Really? I don't think so."

152
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:20,000
It leads to isolation. And now because social media exists, we can isolate by ourselves in a room and we can find a fake community of people online that agree with everything that we agree with.

153
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:29,000
And the end result is immaturity. We're childish. We're in diapers.

154
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:37,000
This is much easier and it's much faster, but it will deform us. It's like eating fast food every single day.

155
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:43,000
Right? Uniformity looks like unity. It's like a Big Mac looks like food.

156
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:47,000
Right?

157
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:54,000
I love me a Big Mac. I'm not saying, but I'm saying if you eat a Big Mac every single day, it has the appearance of nutrients.

158
00:19:54,000 --> 00:20:00,000
It has the appearance of giving you the sustenance that you need, but ultimately it will leave you deformed and unhealthy.

159
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:07,000
And that's the same thing that happens when we regularly choose to leave when conflict arises instead of stay and work through conflict.

160
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:16,000
Now, real quick, before I move on to my next point, I do want to offer some nuance for those of you that have been in some spiritually abusive relationships.

161
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:22,000
Some situations are very unhealthy, and they are worth leaving.

162
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:33,000
Some spiritual leaders will use this message that I'm preaching right now to make you feel like you're the problem and you have to shut up and believe what they say, and if you leave, you're ungodly.

163
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:43,000
And so I want to be careful that there are some situations that are abusive and unhealthy, and it is good to leave those, and it's good to leave those quickly.

164
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:56,000
And there are other situations where you work so hard to reconcile, you sit at the table, you look across the aisle, you talk, you talk, you talk, and ultimately, there's no progress on either side.

165
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:02,000
There are times when you might be called by God to leave that table, but you do so with blessing in your heart.

166
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:08,000
You say, "I love you, brother. You're a brother in Christ. You're a sister in Christ. I love you, but I need to go." And it's probably better for both of us.

167
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:22,000
This happens in the Scriptures. Paul leaves John Mark behind on a missions trip, right? He's like, "This is not working for us." And so there are some situations where we divide, but for the most part, we far too often and far too quickly choose division.

168
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:29,000
And it's causing a church in America that is immature. We might have the appearance of maturity.

169
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:37,000
We might have some Scriptures memorized. We might have some systematic theologies built, but you get us in a room together?

170
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:52,000
We don't act like family because we've divided and we've sectioned off the gospel. We've made the gospel so much smaller than it actually is.

171
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:59,000
And so Paul says that you're actually extremely immature because you're choosing to divide over silly things.

172
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:05,000
It goes on, Paul, in a different letter to the church at Ephesus in Ephesians chapter four, if you want to turn there.

173
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:10,000
So I don't even think I said my main point. I've got so much stuff going on in my head right now.

174
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:18,000
My first point is this, that I put it on the screen. Division is a marker of spiritual immaturity.

175
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:23,000
That when we divide, it demonstrates that we are not as mature as we think we are.

176
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:27,000
The second point I have here is that unity is a marker of spiritual maturity.

177
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:34,000
Ephesians chapter four, starting in verse one. I love that Paul communicates a lot without saying much at all.

178
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:41,000
So first he says, "Brothers and sisters," which is like, "I love you." And then he says, "You're a baby." I love that.

179
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:45,000
And in this passage, he says, "As a prisoner of the Lord."

180
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:51,000
So he's about to give him some more pastoral teaching, but beforehand he's like, "Hey, just remember that I'm in jail for this stuff." Right?

181
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:58,000
I love it. In Corinth, he's like, "Brothers and sisters, you're babies." In Ephesus, he's like, "I'm in jail for you."

182
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:05,000
All right, now let's talk about Jesus. As a prisoner for the Lord, I urge you to live a life worthy of the calling that you have received.

183
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:12,000
He said, "You have been called as a follower of Jesus, as an ambassador of the kingdom of God. You have a calling on your life.

184
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:17,000
Now live according to that calling. Be a doer of the word, not just a hearer only." And what does that look like?

185
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:25,000
Be completely humble and gentle, patient, bearing with one another in love, staying together in love.

186
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:30,000
In the midst of conflict, in the midst of disagreement, you bear together in love.

187
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:38,000
And then he gives a different version of his Romans 12, as far as it is, as long as it's possible for you to live at peace with everyone.

188
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:43,000
He says, "Make every effort to keep the unity of the Spirit through the bond of peace." Why?

189
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:50,000
The verse we read in 1 Corinthians, "There is one body and one Spirit, just as you were called to one hope when you were called.

190
00:23:50,000 --> 00:24:00,000
One Lord, one faith, one baptism, one God and Father over all, who is over all, through all, and in all."

191
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:06,000
Unity isn't a byproduct of spiritual maturity. It's part of the package.

192
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:12,000
So when we work towards maturity, it creates unity.

193
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:22,000
In this type of unity that Paul is inviting us to, it requires humility, gentleness, patience, and sacrificial love, and it requires those things from both parties in the conflict.

194
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:32,000
When conflict and division arise, we both have to set our eyes on Jesus, who is humble, and patient, and kind, and who is working towards reconciliation with all of creation.

195
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:41,000
He is our example, and if He can reconcile all of creation to Himself through His sacrificial love, then we can reconcile brothers and sisters to ourselves

196
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:59,000
through following His example. So looking to Jesus as our example, who is all humble, and gentle, and patient, we approach our brothers and sisters in conflict with that posture.

197
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:10,000
We stay committed to grow with one another, pursuing unity. And here's the thing, and this is the frustrating part for me.

198
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:29,000
Yeah. Sometimes it takes the more mature brother and sister to lead in this. Sometimes we get into division and disagreement, and we're waiting for the other person to come to us to reconcile, and we're both like, "We'll just avoid each other until somebody decides to be the bigger person."

199
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:39,000
And it's actually a sign of maturity if you're willing to go first. And so I just need some of you in the room who have been walking with Jesus for a long time.

200
00:25:39,000 --> 00:26:06,000
I need you all to be spiritually mature when issues of division and conflict arise in our body, and I need you to take the first step. If we want to be a people that build our community around reconciliation with one another rather than division, if we want to create a laboratory of sacrificial love rather than an echo chamber of our own preference, we need those of you in the room who are spiritually mature when you see conflict and division to step in and to push each other closer to relationship with one another.

201
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:16,000
We need to sit across the table from one another. We need to have conversations. We need to learn to listen to one another's perspective. It often requires the most mature person to take the first step.

202
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:33,000
And the thing that I love about this whole process is that disagreements and conflict are, I used to, I'm a, I'm what I call a peacemaker. Now, I used to be a peacekeeper. Those are two different things. That's a totally different sermon.

203
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:44,000
So I don't like conflict, or at least I used to hate conflict and division. But what I've learned as I've grown up in the Lord is that conflict and division is actually an opportunity for discipleship.

204
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:54,000
When conflict and division arise, it allows us this laboratory where we get to sit down together and talk about our disagreements and look to Jesus, the author and perfecter of our faith.

205
00:26:54,000 --> 00:27:01,000
And we get to learn from him how to be united with one another based on sacrificial love that he gives us.

206
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:14,000
And so when conflict arises, don't withdraw, don't get anxious, don't be afraid. Step into it with love as an opportunity for you and the other person or the other group of people to grow closer to Jesus.

207
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:24,000
On the opposite end of our last slide, the cycle of maturity, is when disagreement and conflict arises, we push towards reconciliation.

208
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:29,000
We move towards one another in love. We look each other in the eye. We forgive one another.

209
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:44,000
We repent for our own mistakes so often. Even if you only did like 5% of the mistake making, own it. Repent of the 5% that you did, even if that other person on the other side isn't willing yet to own up to their 95%.

210
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:54,000
You step in. You follow the example of Jesus, and you, you, okay, Jesus didn't need to repent because he didn't sin, so that didn't work there the way I wanted it to.

211
00:27:55,000 --> 00:28:02,000
But you lay down your life for your neighbor, for your brother, for your sister. You take ownership of your mistakes.

212
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:11,000
And this leads to unity, and unity leads to maturity, because when a diverse group of people who are all focused on Jesus are in the same room together,

213
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:21,000
that's when we move more towards the image of Jesus. And I think this is the reason why so many churches in America have so many people that don't really look like Jesus,

214
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:29,000
who have been following Jesus for a really long time, is because we've been filling the room with people who aren't willing to disagree or to work through conflict.

215
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:37,000
And there's churches on every corner. So when conflict arises, you're like, "I'm out. Find another church.

216
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:42,000
That church down the road is looking great. They just had a new logo made. I want to see what's going on in there.

217
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:49,000
They got a fog machine over there. Let's do that." And I saw this this past couple weeks.

218
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:58,000
I literally saw people on social media posting videos of them leaving their church and saying, "I've been going to this church for 10 years and I can't wait to leave and I'm never coming back."

219
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:11,000
That attitude leads to immaturity. I'm not even saying that one side's right or wrong, but that attitude of there's conflict and I'm leaving, that leads to immaturity.

220
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:18,000
What Jesus wants for his church is that we pursue reconciliation in the midst of conflict, no matter how hard it is.

221
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:26,000
If Jesus can die to unify us, we can lay down our preferences to pursue the unity that he died for us to make.

222
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:34,000
It makes us healthier, it makes us stronger.

223
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:43,000
In John Mark's teaching on this, he mentions a study where healthy marriages, he says healthy marriages don't fight less, they just reconcile more.

224
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:54,000
And it's not that we look at these marriages that are healthy, and it's not that they don't have disagreements or fights, it's that they've learned to reconcile with one another in the midst of disagreement, and they grow together through it.

225
00:29:54,000 --> 00:30:01,000
When I was eight years old, playing peewee football like I was an animal, I got some tapes I'll have to show you guys sometime, you come see me.

226
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:07,000
Actually, Ken Sander, can you turn my VHS football tapes into a DVD for me? I'll just put it on the screen next week.

227
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:19,000
I broke my collarbone, and turns out when you break your collarbone, like your body does some stuff to heal it, and for a while it's actually stronger than it was when it broke.

228
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:28,000
So I found out I had like a superhuman collarbone when I was nine years old playing football. That was my best year. It had to be because, but my point here is this, like when...

229
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:37,000
And we go back to the body illustration, right? When your body breaks and you reconcile, it becomes stronger, it becomes more mature.

230
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:44,000
And this is what we've been invited to. I love this quote from Dr. Joseph Hellerman. I'm flying because we're way behind, but it's okay.

231
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:46,000
Marissa, you can come on up, whenever.

232
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:54,000
Dr. Joseph Hellerman says, "The spiritual formation occurs primarily in the context of community.

233
00:30:54,000 --> 00:31:02,000
Persons who remain committed with their brothers and sisters in the local church almost invariably grow in self-understanding.

234
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:08,000
They mature in their ability to relate in healthy ways to God and to their fellow human beings.

235
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:18,000
This is especially the case of those courageous Christians who stick it out through the often messy process of interpersonal discord and conflict resolution.

236
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:24,000
Long-term interpersonal relationships are the crucible of genuine progress in the Christian life.

237
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:30,000
People who stay, grow. People who leave, do not grow.

238
00:31:31,000 --> 00:31:42,000
And this is the thrust of this whole message and this whole invitation from Jesus for this practice is that we've got to stay together around the table because it's in the staying.

239
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:54,000
It's in the reconciling. It's in the working through the conflict that we learn about our own brokenness, that we become aware of the brokenness in others, and that we continue to look to Jesus who unites us.

240
00:31:55,000 --> 00:32:02,000
If we want to grow in spiritual maturity, we've got to stay committed to unity and repair in the midst of conflict and disagreement.

241
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:15,000
Which leads me to my main point, my overarching point for you note takers. I think this is the longest main point I've ever had in my entire life, which probably isn't a good preaching technique.

242
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:22,000
If it's longer than my main point when I was a 20-year-old preacher, probably not a good preaching technique, but here we are anyway.

243
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:35,000
The unified community is the slow, sacred fruit of the gospel, where repentance softens hearts, forgiveness heals wounds, and love matures us into the image of Jesus.

244
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:51,000
Coming together in unity, it's a fruit that happens as we set our eyes on Jesus, as we lay down our preferences, as we look our brothers and sisters in the eye after they hurt us, after they disagree with us, after they post an opinion on social

245
00:32:51,000 --> 00:33:04,000
media that we can't even imagine how they would have ever gotten to believe that. And we don't walk away from them. We sit down at the table and we have a conversation and we listen. And we don't listen to respond, we listen to understand.

246
00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:11,000
And we allow the Holy Spirit to use these conversations to form us into the image of Jesus.

247
00:33:11,000 --> 00:33:17,000
This type of community has not happened by accident or overnight.

248
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:22,000
We will naturally drift towards division and echo chambers and isolation. It's easier.

249
00:33:22,000 --> 00:33:25,000
And we as human beings love to do what's easy.

250
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:33,000
Division happens by impulse, but unity happens through intentionality.

251
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:42,000
And that's the thing that we have to remember when conflict arises. There's such a, there's a point when conflict arises where that fight or flight thing in our brain starts firing.

252
00:33:45,000 --> 00:33:46,000
And we just want to run.

253
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:53,000
And oftentimes we do so much damage in the moment of conflict, the things that come out of our mouth, the things that we say that we don't mean.

254
00:33:53,000 --> 00:33:58,000
And then we're, we're too prideful to come back and to seek reconciliation and to repent.

255
00:33:58,000 --> 00:34:11,000
So we've got to, we've got to create a, we want to create a culture here at Canvas where reconciliation is accepted.

256
00:34:12,000 --> 00:34:13,000
It's expected.

257
00:34:13,000 --> 00:34:28,000
It's a sacred partnership because it's only by God's grace that we can remain united.

258
00:34:28,000 --> 00:34:31,000
It's not something that you and I can accomplish on our own.

259
00:34:31,000 --> 00:34:36,000
It's not something that we could do conflict resolution classes and get to.

260
00:34:36,000 --> 00:34:39,000
The reason we are united is because of the blood of Jesus.

261
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:47,000
Notice the passage of Scripture doesn't say, "Be eager to cultivate the unity of spirit and the bond of peace."

262
00:34:47,000 --> 00:34:50,000
It says, "Be eager to keep the unity."

263
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:54,000
Unity is a gift that's given to us by the blood of Jesus.

264
00:34:54,000 --> 00:34:57,000
It's not our responsibility to make it.

265
00:34:57,000 --> 00:35:03,000
It's our responsibility to keep it by following the example of Jesus.

266
00:35:06,000 --> 00:35:13,000
Unity is a gift that Jesus gives us, not because we deserve it or we're able to keep it without Him, but because this is His vision for the world.

267
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:23,000
You guys want to be five minutes late to lunch? Thank you.

268
00:35:23,000 --> 00:35:25,000
Sorry.

269
00:35:25,000 --> 00:35:29,000
I said I'm a planner. I like to plan. I like to get you guys. I like, anyway. All right.

270
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:43,000
John Mark and his teaching on this was talking about visiting an abbot where the spiritual people commit to live there for their entire lives.

271
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:49,000
And it's just a bunch of religious dudes that built this incredible retreat center for prayer.

272
00:35:49,000 --> 00:35:56,000
And John Mark said, "How do a bunch of religious people make such a nice retreat center?"

273
00:35:56,000 --> 00:36:02,000
And Abbot Jeremy said this. He says, "When you know you're going to die in a place,

274
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:04,000
you tend to take really good care of it."

275
00:36:04,000 --> 00:36:08,000
And when I heard that quote, I couldn't help but think this,

276
00:36:08,000 --> 00:36:13,000
that knowing our destination, our eternal destination impacts the way that we live today.

277
00:36:13,000 --> 00:36:21,000
And when I read the Scriptures, I read about every tribe, nation, language and tongue surrounded,

278
00:36:21,000 --> 00:36:27,000
surrounding the throne of God, worshiping King Jesus, united as one group of people.

279
00:36:27,000 --> 00:36:31,000
And if that is where we are going, then we better start cultivating that now.

280
00:36:31,000 --> 00:36:46,000
If our destination is unified, surrendered to King Jesus, with people that have different opinions than we do, with people who committed sins that we can't even imagine that Jesus would have redeemed them in the midst of their sinfulness and their failures.

281
00:36:46,000 --> 00:36:54,000
If we can't unify around people that are broken and messed up and have wrong opinions now, then what is eternity going to look like for us?

282
00:36:54,000 --> 00:36:57,000
This is where we're headed.

283
00:36:58,000 --> 00:37:19,000
And knowing our destination changes our earthly efforts. We are headed to a kingdom where God sits on His throne, and all of our preferences and all of our failures fade away so that we can circle His throne as His people from every tribe, language, and tongue, and we can celebrate Him who is worthy, for He is slain, and He is the one who unites us.

284
00:37:26,000 --> 00:37:48,000
So, a couple of practical things. Sit down when you're tempted to withdraw. When you feel like you can't even imagine how that brother or sister could believe that, think that, say that, do that. Sit down at the table. Be curious. Listen to understand and not to respond. Look to Jesus.

285
00:37:49,000 --> 00:38:07,000
And stay at the table long enough for conflicts to arise and disagreements to form. And do the slow, hard work of reconciliation. Repent. Forgive. Follow the example of Jesus. Grow in humility, patience, gentleness, and sacrificial love. And make every effort to maintain the unity of the Spirit through the bond of peace.

286
00:38:08,000 --> 00:38:29,000
Imagine, with me, if this is what our community was known for. If we refused to settle for Ecker Chambers of preference and instead cultivated a laboratory of sacrificial love that resulted in spiritual maturity. Where we become aware of our blind spots and learn from our brothers and sisters who have different perspectives than we do.

287
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:52,000
Where we lay down our preferences and our own packaging of the gospel for the gospel of the kingdom of God. Where we take ownership of how we've contributed to conflict and division and we repent. And where we work hard to extend forgiveness to those who have offended us, not because they deserve it, but because Jesus forgave us when we didn't. This is the gospel.

288
00:38:54,000 --> 00:39:11,000
And our theology is only as good as the love that it produces and the community that it creates. May we be a people committed to staying together at the table. Though the world is obsessed with division and allergic to reconciliation. May we be a city on a hill.

289
00:39:11,000 --> 00:39:18,000
Let's not put the basket over our lantern. Let's shine a light in the darkness. Let's be a people who remain unified around the gospel.

290
00:39:20,000 --> 00:39:23,000
I'm going to pray. Marissa is going to do whatever the Spirit told Marissa to do.

291
00:39:23,000 --> 00:39:30,000
The Spirit told Marissa to do nothing. I'm going to pray.

292
00:39:30,000 --> 00:39:43,000
And then as I'm praying, if you are like, "I need to be baptized today," then we're going to do it, right?

293
00:39:43,000 --> 00:39:49,000
You guys want to be 10 minutes late to lunch? All right. So I'll pray.

294
00:39:49,000 --> 00:39:51,000
We're still doing whatever the Spirit told her to do.

295
00:39:51,000 --> 00:39:54,000
If the Spirit's telling you that it's time to be baptized, we're going to do that.

296
00:39:54,000 --> 00:39:59,000
And if that doesn't happen, then we're going to go to lunch eight minutes late and praise the Lord.

297
00:39:59,000 --> 00:40:07,000
Father in heaven, we are one body, united by one Spirit.

298
00:40:07,000 --> 00:40:12,000
We have one Lord, one faith, and one baptism that unites us all.

299
00:40:12,000 --> 00:40:18,000
And it unites all of us, red, yellow, black, and white.

300
00:40:18,000 --> 00:40:21,000
We are precious in your sight.

301
00:40:21,000 --> 00:40:29,000
We have strong opinions about emotionally charged issues.

302
00:40:29,000 --> 00:40:36,000
We allow the gospel of the kingdom of God and the Holy Spirit to inform those opinions,

303
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:39,000
and yet sometimes we still make errors and mistakes.

304
00:40:39,000 --> 00:40:45,000
We put too many eggs in the wrong basket, and we unite as a family of God

305
00:40:45,000 --> 00:40:48,000
so that we can point out each other's blind spots and grow.

306
00:40:48,000 --> 00:40:57,000
God, may this church be a church full of people that become more and more mature into the image of Jesus,

307
00:40:57,000 --> 00:41:02,000
because when conflict and division arise, we step into it.

308
00:41:02,000 --> 00:41:10,000
We work towards reconciliation, because we know where we're headed.

309
00:41:13,000 --> 00:41:18,000
And whether we like it or not, we are all going to be around your throne one day, all of us who have surrendered to Jesus.

310
00:41:18,000 --> 00:41:23,000
So if we can't stay in the room, if we can't be in a room together now, eternity is not going to be as fun as we think it is.

311
00:41:23,000 --> 00:41:30,000
God, may we move towards our eternal destiny here and now, for your glory, for our good, and for the good of our neighbor.

312
00:41:30,000 --> 00:41:32,000
In Jesus name I pray. Amen.
